Dynamic programming is often used to optimize algorithms by storing the results of subproblems to avoid redundant computations. This approach significantly reduces the computational complexity and enhances performance, particularly for problems that exhibit both optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems.

In contrast, a naive recursive algorithm may suffer from exponential time complexity due to repeated computation of the same subproblems. Dynamic programming addresses this issue by caching the results of subproblems, thereby achieving polynomial time complexity in many cases.

